they have not swept.","Choose **have not swept**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Place **not after have** and keep **swept**: they have not swept.","B":"Perfect negatives do not add did; not follows the existing auxiliary.","C":"The -ing form needs been for a perfect continuous, which is not requested.","D":"In the standard finite negative, not follows the auxiliary."}}},{"id":"14008f60-a67c-5e82-af31-53cb001d051e","topicSlug":"present-perfect","level":"B1","prompt":"Begin this present-perfect question: ____ they swept the kitchen floor up to now?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Has"},{"id":"B","text":"Have"},{"id":"C","text":"Did"},{"id":"D","text":"Are"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The first auxiliary moves before the subject; do not add a new auxiliary.","explanation":"Move **have** before they. The participle swept remains after the subject.","keywords":["perfect-question"],"skill":"perfect-question","contextKey":"present-perfect-50","fingerprint":"053a11e762418875aac8d01fda60503789811dc1f882e85dc4e2f31434c53f71","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-present-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The first auxiliary moves before the subject; do not add a new auxiliary.","Move **have** before they. The participle swept remains after the subject.","Choose **Have**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Has does not agree with they, and cannot form a past-perfect question.","B":"Move **have** before they. The participle swept remains after the subject.","C":"Did would require a base verb and would not make this perfect question.","D":"Are would create a different structure, not the requested perfect."}}},{"id":"8faf40d3-7a06-517f-afc2-1d7539b59f9f","topicSlug":"present-perfect","level":"B1","prompt":"Choose the participle for the present perfect: We have ____ the kitchen floor up to now. (sweep)","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to sweep"},{"id":"B","text":"been sweeping"},{"id":"C","text":"swept"},{"id":"D","text":"sweeping"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Identify the past participle, rather than choosing a word only because it looks past.","explanation":"The past participle of **sweep** is **swept**. It follows have in the requested perfect construction.","keywords":["perfect-participle"],"skill":"perfect-participle","contextKey":"present-perfect-50","fingerprint":"6877023c4496bfed33b34431a568a4410e5f7a38b81c46e13798790bda228326","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-present-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Identify the past participle, rather than choosing a word only because it looks past.","The past participle of **sweep** is **swept**. It follows have in the requested perfect construction.","Choose **swept**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This is the to-infinitive, not a past participle.","B":"This would make a perfect continuous construction, not perfect simple.","C":"The past participle of **sweep** is **swept**. It follows have in the requested perfect construction.","D":"This is the -ing form, not the past participle."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["The present perfect connects earlier events with the present: an experience so far, a recent result or a period that is still open. Contrast this with a finished past time such as yesterday. A past participle may resemble the past simple, but irregular verbs can differ: went / gone, saw / seen.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Connect two points in time","explanation":"The present perfect connects earlier events with the present: an experience so far, a recent result or a period that is still open. Contrast this with a finished past time such as yesterday. A past participle may resemble the past simple, but irregular verbs can differ: went / gone, saw / seen.","pattern":"Subject + **have / has + past participle**","commonMistakes":["For a finished date, use past simple: I visited last year. British English often prefers present perfect with just or already; American English also uses past simple in those contexts."],"examples":["Use the present perfect: She **has prepared** the visitor guide up to now.","Use the present perfect: She **has restored** the old portrait up to now."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Build the perfect verb","explanation":"A perfect construction uses **have as an auxiliary**, followed by a past participle. Regular participles normally end in -ed. Irregular ones must be learned with their base and past forms: write / wrote / written. Do not substitute an -ing form unless you also build the continuous construction.","pattern":"She **has written**. / They **have written**.","commonMistakes":["The form written is a participle; wrote is past simple. They are not interchangeable after have."],"examples":["Make a present-perfect negative: They **have not prepared** the visitor guide up to now.","Make a present-perfect negative: They **have not restored** the old portrait up to now."],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Negatives and questions","explanation":"Put **not after have or has**. For a yes/no question, move the auxiliary before the subject. The participle stays unchanged. There is no extra do or did because the perfect construction already contains an auxiliary.","pattern":"Have they finished? / They **have not finished**.","commonMistakes":["Do not combine did with a perfect auxiliary to make its negative or question."],"examples":["Begin this present-perfect question: **Have** they prepared the visitor guide up to now?","Begin this present-perfect question: **Have** they restored the old portrait up to now?"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Time expressions and short answers","explanation":"Read the whole sentence to locate the reference time. Here it is **up to now**. Keep the appropriate form of have in a short answer and omit the repeated participle. Since names a starting point; for names a duration. Neither word automatically selects a single tense.","pattern":"Yes, they **have**. / No, they **have not**.","commonMistakes":["Been can describe a completed visit; gone often means the person has left and has not yet returned."],"examples":["Choose the participle for the present perfect: We have **prepared** the visitor guide up to now. (prepare)","Choose the participle for the present perfect: We have **restored** the old portrait up to now. (restore)"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["Subject + **have / has + past participle**","She **has written**. / They **have written**.","Have they finished? / They **have not finished**.","Yes, they **have**. / No, they **have not**."]},"stage":"3 · Time and experience","prerequisites":["future-forms"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
